From 710e6ad12af6d5fba4c4775916e1039f58d02b4c Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: rkvenc forward-port <forward-port@local>
Date: Fri, 24 Jul 2026 19:06:57 -0700
Subject: [PATCH 74/87] video: rockchip: rga: derive 10-bit UV plane offsets
 byte-literally

Commit 138f0de2c972 ("video: rockchip: rga3: honor the legacy byte-stride
ABI for 10-bit rasters") restored the BSP contract that rga_req.img.vir_w
carries a BYTE stride for 10-bit formats, and converted the RGA3 register
writer accordingly.  It missed the sibling site: rga_convert_addr() still
scaled vir_w by the pixel depth (x10/8 compact, x2 incompact) when deriving
the UV plane offset, so the depth is now double-applied one site over --
exactly the error that commit fixed in the stride writer.

The Y plane of a 10-bit semi-planar surface is vir_w * vir_h bytes, for
compact NV15/NV20 and incompact P010/P210 alike.  Scaling it places the UV
plane past the true one, which fails two ways:

  - a tightly sized surface faults, because the UV read runs off the end of
    the mapping (rk_iommu read fault, job abort, EACCES to userspace -- the
    same signature that commit was meant to remove); and

  - an over-sized surface does NOT fault and silently returns chroma read
    from the wrong offset, which is worse: no error reaches userspace.

Measured on a booted RK3588 with a forced-core legacy RGA_BLIT_SYNC of a
compact NV12_10 320x240 surface, vir_w = 448 (byte stride), below-4G CMA
dma-bufs.  Bisecting the source allocation that stops the fault gives
188160 B = 560*240 + 448*120 -- a pixel-scaled Y plane offset over
byte-literal UV rows -- against the 161280 B the surface actually needs.
The incompact branch gives 268800 B = 896*240 + 448*120, predicted from the
x2 scaling before it was measured.  With the source over-allocated so that
neither offset can fault, the blit succeeds and the destination chroma is
read entirely from the scaled offset, confirming the wrong-output path.

Drop the scaling and the now-redundant compact_mode branch.  10-bit formats
stay semi-planar, so v_addr remains zero.

Fixes: 6c7eb3efa3f0 ("video: rockchip: rga3: derive 10-bit plane offsets byte-literally")
---
 drivers/video/rockchip/rga3/rga_common.c | 25 +++++++++++-------------
 1 file changed, 11 insertions(+), 14 deletions(-)

diff --git a/drivers/video/rockchip/rga3/rga_common.c b/drivers/video/rockchip/rga3/rga_common.c
index 865c9edd8a974..f100894dff638 100644
--- a/drivers/video/rockchip/rga3/rga_common.c
+++ b/drivers/video/rockchip/rga3/rga_common.c
@@ -722,21 +722,18 @@ void rga_convert_addr(struct rga_img_info_t *img, bool before_vir_get_channel)
 	if (img->rd_mode != RGA_FBC_MODE || before_vir_get_channel) {
 		if (rga_is_yuv10bit_format(img->format)) {
 			/*
-			 * 10-bit semi-planar rows are byte-literal: incompact
-			 * P010/P210 rows carry 16-bit containers (2 bytes per
-			 * pixel), compact NV15/NV20 rows pack 10 bits per
-			 * pixel. The 1 byte/pixel offset below would place the
-			 * UV plane inside the Y plane.
+			 * 10-bit vir_w already IS the row size in bytes (the
+			 * legacy BSP ABI contract honoured by the RGA2 writer
+			 * and restored on RGA3 by commit 138f0de2c972), so the
+			 * Y plane is vir_w * vir_h bytes for compact NV15/NV20
+			 * and incompact P010/P210 alike.  Scaling by the pixel
+			 * depth here (x10/8 or x2) double-applies it and pushes
+			 * the UV plane past the true one -- reading chroma from
+			 * the wrong offset, and running off the end of a
+			 * tightly sized surface.
 			 */
-			uint64_t y_bytes;
-
-			if (img->compact_mode == RGA_10BIT_INCOMPACT)
-				y_bytes = (uint64_t)img->vir_w * img->vir_h * 2;
-			else
-				y_bytes =
-					(uint64_t)img->vir_w * img->vir_h * 10 / 8;
-
-			img->uv_addr = img->yrgb_addr + y_bytes;
+			img->uv_addr = img->yrgb_addr +
+				       (u64)img->vir_w * img->vir_h;
 			/* All 10-bit formats are semi-planar: no third plane. */
 			img->v_addr = 0;
 			return;
